  <title>7.8.4: Writing Equivalent Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01874</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.4: Writing Equivalent Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7da1acbd-cd14-4056-b1a6-bcb2bfe56d1f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Draw a diagram to show that \((3x+1)(x+2)\) is equivalent to \(3x^2+7x+2\).</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>Diagram should show partial products of \(3x(x)\), \(6x\), \(x\), and 2, which add up to \(3x^2+7x+2\).</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7cd83e7946410aa753c891045d8c29dbbf01b4cd"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Is \((x+4)^2\) equivalent to \(2x^2+8x+8\)? Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>No. \((x+4)(x+4)=x^2+4x+4x+16=x^2+8x+16\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Interaction End -->
<div class="os-raise-student-reflection">
  <para class="os-raise-student-reflection-title">Why Should I Care?</para>
  <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7536812b8dcff5334ed90bc5fda8a2a19521a7a2"/></media></figure><para>Anna's class entered a contest where they had to build a catapault and use it to launch a pumpkin at a distant target. But when Anna's class shot their pumpkin for the first time, it went wildly off course.</para>
  <para>Anna thought about their problem and came up with a solution. If Anna's class used a quadratic equation, they could mathematically find the best place and angle for their pumpkin catapault. It worked! Their accuracy went from 2% to 99%. </para>
  <para>You can use quadratic equations for any object that travels in a curved path, like footballs, frisbees, and golf balls. </para>
</div></section>
</content>
